\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Two sources in one loop.} A 6 V battery and a 12 V battery are connected in the same series loop with a $2\,\Omega$ and a $4\,\Omega$ resistor, arranged so the two batteries drive current the same way round. Find the loop current, then the p.d. across the point pair $PQ$ that spans the $2\,\Omega$ resistor and the 6 V battery together.

\textbf{Solution.} When sources aid each other their emfs add; when they oppose, subtract. Here they aid, so the net emf is $6+12=18$ V around a total resistance $2+4=6\,\Omega$:
\[
I=\frac{\text{net emf}}{\text{total }R}=\frac{18}{6}=3.0\text{ A}.
\]
Across $PQ$, the $2\,\Omega$ resistor drops $IR=3.0\times2=6$ V while the 6 V battery raises the potential by 6 V in the opposite sense, so
\[
V_{PQ}=6-6=\boxed{0\text{ V}}.
\]
Always fix a direction round the loop first, then add emfs that push that way and subtract those that push back.
\end{exercise}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=green!5,leftline=true,linecolor=green!60!black,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Cells and capacity}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item Identical cells \textbf{in series} add emf ($n$ cells give $n\mathcal E$). \textbf{In parallel} the emf is unchanged (still $\mathcal E$), but they last longer.
  \item Battery capacity in ampere-hours is a charge: $1\text{ A h}=1\text{ A}\times3600\text{ s}=3600$ C. Energy available is $W=VQ=V(It)$. Example: a 11.1 V, 4.8 A h pack stores $11.1\times4.8\times3600\approx1.9\times10^{5}$ J.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

