\section{Current and Charge}

\textbf{What is current?} Current ($I$) is the \emph{rate of flow of electric charge} — how much charge passes a point in a wire per second. Its unit is the ampere (A), where $1\text{ A} = 1\text{ C s}^{-1}$.

\textbf{What does ``energy changes form'' mean?} When charge flows through a device such as a lamp, it transfers energy. The charge arrives with \emph{high electrical energy} and leaves with \emph{less} — the missing energy has been converted into light and heat. The charge itself is \textbf{not used up}; it keeps circulating. The total energy is conserved, just transformed into a different form.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % --- wire with arrow (current label) ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (-1.5, 0) -- (-0.6, 0) node[midway, above] {$I$};

    % --- lamp box ---
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=2cm, minimum height=1.2cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] (lamp) at (0.8, 0) {lamp};

    % --- wire after lamp ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (2.5, 0) -- (3.4, 0) node[midway, above] {$I$ (same)};

    % --- energy label: entering (high) ---
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, red!70!black]
      at (-1.6, 0.4) {$E$ high};
    \node[anchor=north east, font=\footnotesize, red!70!black]
      at (-1.6, -0.35) {(electrical)};

    % --- energy label: leaving (low) ---
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, red!70!black]
      at (3.5, 0.4) {$E$ low};
    \node[anchor=north west, font=\footnotesize, red!70!black]
      at (3.5, -0.35) {(light + heat)};

    % --- light arrow ---
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin]
      (1.3, 0.6) .. controls +(0.3, 0.5) .. +(0.8, 0.5)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {light};
    % --- heat arrow ---
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin]
      (0.3, -0.6) .. controls +(0.3, -0.45) .. +(0.8, -0.45)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {heat};
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Charge is conserved} — the same amount of charge that enters the lamp leaves it.
  \item \textbf{Current is the same everywhere} in a series circuit — the rate of charge flow does not slow down inside a component.
  \item \textbf{Energy changes form} — electrical energy is transformed into light and thermal energy inside the lamp.
\end{itemize}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A lamp carries a steady current of $0.30$ A for 2 minutes. Find the charge passing through it. Does the lamp use up charge?


\textbf{Solution.} Current is the rate of flow of charge:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{Q}{t}} \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad Q=It.
\]
Convert time first: $2$ min $=120$ s. Therefore
\[
Q=(0.30)(120)=\boxed{36\text{ C}}.
\]
The lamp does \emph{not} use up charge. In steady operation, 36 C enters the lamp and 36 C leaves it. The lamp transfers electrical energy to light and thermal energy.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} $1.88\times10^{18}$ electrons flow through a resistor each second. Find the current. (Electron charge magnitude $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C.)

\textbf{Solution.} Total charge per second is the number of electrons times the charge on each:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{ne}{t}} \qquad I=\frac{(1.88\times10^{18})(1.60\times10^{-19})}{1}=\boxed{0.301\text{ A}}.
\]
Here $n$ is a pure count of electrons, so $Q=ne$ before dividing by time. Watch the powers of ten: $10^{18}\times10^{-19}=10^{-1}$.
\end{exercise}

\subsection{Direction of Current}

Conventional current is defined as the direction that \emph{positive charge} would flow — from the positive ($+$) terminal of a battery, through the circuit, to the negative ($-$) terminal. In most circuits, the actual moving charges are \emph{electrons}, which carry negative charge and therefore move in the \emph{opposite direction}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,line width=0.9pt]
  % ---- Circuit loop (battery gap on the left between y=1.3 and y=1.7) ----
  \draw (0,1.7) -- (0,3) -- (5,3) -- (5,0) -- (0,0) -- (0,1.3);

  % ---- Battery on the left side (long plate = +, short thick plate = -) ----
  \draw[line width=1pt]   (-0.4,1.7) -- (0.4,1.7);
  \draw[line width=2.6pt] (-0.22,1.3) -- (0.22,1.3);
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.72) {$+$};
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.28) {$-$};
  \node[left,align=center,font=\footnotesize] at (-0.75,1.5) {battery};

  % ---- Lamp on the right side ----
  \draw[fill=white] (5,1.5) circle (0.45);
  \draw (5-0.34,1.5) arc (180:360:0.34);
  \node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5.6,1.5) {lamp};

  % ---- Conventional current: blue, out of + terminal, clockwise ----
  \draw[->,blue!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (0.6,3.42) -- (4.4,3.42);
  \node[blue!70!black,above,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,3.5) {conventional current $I$ (+ to $-$)};

  % ---- Electron flow: red, opposite direction ----
  \draw[->,red!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (4.4,2.58) -- (0.6,2.58);
  \node[red!70!black,below,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,2.5) {electron flow ($-$ to $+$)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=red!5,leftline=true,linecolor=red,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Common traps}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item Use the magnitude of electron charge, $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C, when finding current magnitude.
  \item Convert minutes to seconds and mA to A before using $Q=It$.
  \item Charges do not move more slowly after a resistor in a steady series circuit. The current is the same at every point in that path.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Parallel Circuits}

In a \textbf{parallel circuit}, components are connected side-by-side on separate branches. Charge splits between the branches, but the total charge is conserved.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % --- Main wire from battery (left) ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (-1.5, 1.5) -- (-0.5, 1.5) node[midway, above] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % --- Junction: split ---
    \fill (0, 1.5) circle (0.08);
    \draw[thick] (0, 1.5) -- (0, 0.5);

    % --- Top branch: wire to lamp 1 ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (0, 1.5) -- (2.5, 1.5) node[midway, above] {$I_1$} to[candle, pos=0.5] (4.5, 1.5);
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.8cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.5, 1.5) {lamp 1};

    % --- Bottom branch: wire to lamp 2 ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (0, 0.5) -- (2.5, 0.5) node[midway, above] {$I_2$} to[candle, pos=0.5] (4.5, 0.5);
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.8cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.5, 0.5) {lamp 2};

    % --- Junction: rejoin ---
    \fill (5, 1.5) circle (0.08);
    \draw[thick] (5, 1.5) -- (5, 0.5);

    % --- Wire back to battery (right) ---
    \draw[thick, ->] (5, 1.5) -- (5.8, 1.5);
    \draw[thick] (5.8, 1.5) -- (6.0, 1.5) node[midway, above] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % --- Battery on left ---
    \draw[thick] (-2.0, 0.8) rectangle (-1.3, 2.2);
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-2.1, 1.5) {battery};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 2.0) {$+$};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 1.0) {$-};

    % --- Annotations: current values ---
    \node[anchor=south, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.5, 2.2) {$I_1 = \dfrac{I_{\text{total}}}{2}$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.5, 0.0) {$I_2 = \dfrac{I_{\text{total}}}{2}$};

    % --- Key insight ---
    \node[draw, dashed, rounded corners, fill=green!5, inner sep=5pt, font=\footnotesize, align=left] at (3.0, -0.8)
      {\parbox{5.5cm}{
        \textbullet\ Each branch gets part of the total current. \\
        \textbullet\ The current through each lamp is \emph{less} than $I_{\text{total}}$. \\
        \textbullet\ If both lamps are identical, $I_1 = I_2 = I_{\text{total}}/2$.}};
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

