\section{Current and Charge}

\textbf{What is current?} Current ($I$) is the \emph{rate of flow of electric charge} — how much charge passes a point in a wire per second. The unit is the ampere (A), where $1\text{ A} = 1\text{ C s}^{-1}$.

\textbf{What does ``energy changes form'' mean?} When charge flows through a device such as a lamp, it transfers energy. The charge arrives with \emph{high electrical energy} and leaves with \emph{less} — the missing energy has been converted into light and heat. The charge itself is \textbf{not used up}; it keeps circulating. Total energy is conserved, just transformed into a different form.

\textbf{Charge is conserved.} In a steady circuit, the same amount of charge that enters a component leaves it. Current is the same at every point in a series circuit — the rate of charge flow does not slow down inside a component.

\medskip
\textbf{Series circuit diagram.} The diagram below shows a complete series circuit. Charge flows out of the positive ($+$) terminal of the battery, through the lamp, and back into the negative ($-$) terminal.

