\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Water pump} $\longleftrightarrow$ \textbf{Battery} — provides the ``push'' (pressure / voltage) to make water / charge flow.
  \item \textbf{Turbine / water wheel} $\longleftrightarrow$ \textbf{Lamp / resistor} — converts the flow's energy into another form (kinetic $\rightarrow$ electrical $\rightarrow$ light/heat).
  \item \textbf{Pipes} $\longleftrightarrow$ \textbf{Wires} — channels through which the stuff flows (with some resistance).
  \item \textbf{Flow rate} $\longleftrightarrow$ \textbf{Current ($I$)} — how much water / charge passes a point per second.
  \item \textbf{Pressure difference} $\longleftrightarrow$ \textbf{Potential difference ($V$)} — the driving ``push'' between two points.
\end{itemize}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A lamp carries a steady current of $0.30$ A for 2 minutes. Find the charge passing through it. Does the lamp use up charge?


\textbf{Solution.} Current is the rate of flow of charge:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{Q}{t}} \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad Q=It.
\]
Convert time first: $2$ min $=120$ s. Therefore
\[
Q=(0.30)(120)=\boxed{36\text{ C}}.
\]
The lamp does \emph{not} use up charge. In steady operation, 36 C enters the lamp and 36 C leaves it. The lamp transfers electrical energy to light and thermal energy.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} $1.88\times10^{18}$ electrons flow through a resistor each second. Find the current. (Electron charge magnitude $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C.)

\textbf{Solution.} Total charge per second is the number of electrons times the charge on each:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{ne}{t}} \qquad I=\frac{(1.88\times10^{18})(1.60\times10^{-19})}{1}=\boxed{0.301\text{ A}}.
\]
Here $n$ is a pure count of electrons, so $Q=ne$ before dividing by time. Watch the powers of ten: $10^{18}\times10^{-19}=10^{-1}$.
\end{exercise}

\subsection{Direction of Current}

Conventional current is defined as the direction that \emph{positive charge} would flow — from the positive ($+$) terminal of a battery, through the circuit, to the negative ($-$) terminal. In most circuits, the actual moving charges are \emph{electrons}, which carry negative charge and therefore move in the \emph{opposite direction}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,line width=0.9pt]
  % ---- Circuit loop ----
  \draw (0,1.7) -- (0,3) -- (5,3) -- (5,0) -- (0,0) -- (0,1.3);

  % ---- Battery ----
  \draw[line width=1pt]   (-0.4,1.7) -- (0.4,1.7);
  \draw[line width=2.6pt] (-0.22,1.3) -- (0.22,1.3);
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.72) {$+$};
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.28) {$-$};
  \node[left,align=center,font=\footnotesize] at (-0.75,1.5) {battery};

  % ---- Lamp ----
  \draw[fill=white] (5,1.5) circle (0.45);
  \draw (5-0.34,1.5) arc (180:360:0.34);
  \node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5.6,1.5) {lamp};

  % ---- Conventional current: blue, out of + terminal, clockwise ----
  \draw[->,blue!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (0.6,3.42) -- (4.4,3.42);
  \node[blue!70!black,above,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,3.5) {conventional current $I$ (+ to $-$)};

  % ---- Electron flow: red, opposite direction ----
  \draw[->,red!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (4.4,2.58) -- (0.6,2.58);
  \node[red!70!black,below,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,2.5) {electron flow ($-$ to $+$)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=red!5,leftline=true,linecolor=red,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Common traps}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item Use the magnitude of electron charge, $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C, when finding current magnitude.
  \item Convert minutes to seconds and mA to A before using $Q=It$.
  \item Charges do not move more slowly after a resistor in a steady series circuit. The current is the same at every point in that path.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Parallel Circuits}

In a \textbf{parallel circuit}, components are connected side-by-side on separate branches. Charge splits between the branches — each branch receives part of the total charge. The total charge is still conserved.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % ---- Circuit frame ----
    % Top wire: left to right
    \draw[thick] (-2, 2) -- (0, 2);
    % Bottom wire: left to right
    \draw[thick] (-2, 0) -- (0, 0);
    % Bottom return: right to left
    \draw[thick, ->] (4.5, 0) -- (4.5, 2) -- (3.5, 2) node[midway, above] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % ---- Junction dots ----
    \fill (0, 2) circle (0.1) node[above left] {};
    \fill (4.5, 2) circle (0.1);

    % ---- Top branch: wire + lamp 1 ----
    \draw[thick] (0, 2) -- (1.8, 2) node[midway, above, font=\footnotesize] {$I_1$};
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.5cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.7, 2) {lamp 1};
    \draw[thick] (3.6, 2) -- (4.5, 2);

    % ---- Bottom branch: wire + lamp 2 ----
    \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (1.8, 0) node[midway, below, font=\footnotesize] {$I_2$};
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.5cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.7, 0) {lamp 2};
    \draw[thick] (3.6, 0) -- (4.5, 0);

    % ---- Battery on the left vertical wire ----
    \draw[thick] (-2.0, 0.8) rectangle (-1.3, 1.2);
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-2.1, 1.0) {battery};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 1.2) {$+$};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 0.8) {$-};

    % ---- Incoming / outgoing current arrows ----
    \draw[thick, ->] (-2.8, 2) -- (-2.1, 2) node[midway, above, font=\footnotesize] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % ---- Annotation: current splitting ----
    \node[anchor=south, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.7, 2.9) {$I_1 = I_{\text{total}}/2$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.7, -0.3) {$I_2 = I_{\text{total}}/2$};

    % ---- Junction dots bottom ----
    \fill (0, 0) circle (0.1);
    \fill (4.5, 0) circle (0.1);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Each branch receives part of the total current: $I_1 + I_2 = I_{\text{total}}$.
  \item If both lamps are identical (equal resistance), the current splits equally: $I_1 = I_2 = I_{\text{total}}/2$.
  \item The current through each lamp is \emph{less} than the total current.
  \item In the water analogy: one pipe splits into two smaller pipes; the total water flow rate is conserved, but each branch carries a fraction.
\end{itemize}

