\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A 3.0 V battery supplies a $4\,\Omega$ resistor in series with a parallel pair of $1\,\Omega$ and $2\,\Omega$. Find the current through the $1\,\Omega$ resistor.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (0,2.4) -- (1,2.4);
  \draw[thick] (1,2.05) rectangle (2.5,2.75) node[pos=.5] {$4\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick,red] (2.5,2.4) -- (3.2,2.4) -- (3.2,3.1) -- (3.8,3.1);
  \draw[thick] (3.8,2.75) rectangle (5.3,3.45) node[pos=.5] {$1\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick,green!60!black] (5.3,3.1) -- (5.9,3.1) -- (5.9,2.4);
  \draw[thick,red] (3.2,2.4) -- (3.2,1.7) -- (3.8,1.7);
  \draw[thick] (3.8,1.35) rectangle (5.3,2.05) node[pos=.5] {$2\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick,green!60!black] (5.3,1.7) -- (5.9,1.7) -- (5.9,2.4) -- (6.6,2.4) -- (6.6,0) -- (1.1,0);
  \draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (0.75,0);
  \draw[thick] (0.75,0.15) -- (0.75,-0.15);
  \draw[very thick] (1.1,0.3) -- (1.1,-0.3);
  \node[below] at (0.93,-0.35) {3.0 V};
  \node[blue,above] at (0.45,1.15) {node A};
  \node[red,above] at (3.2,3.25) {node B};
  \node[green!60!black,above] at (6.25,1.15) {node C};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Blue, red, and green wires mark three different potentials. Both parallel resistors connect from red node B to green node C, so they must have the same p.d.

First reduce the parallel pair:
\[
R_p=\frac{(1)(2)}{1+2}=\frac{2}{3}\,\Omega,
\qquad R_{\rm total}=4+\frac{2}{3}=\frac{14}{3}\,\Omega.
\]
Thus $I_{\rm total}=3/(14/3)=9/14$ A. The p.d. across the parallel pair is
\[
V_p=I_{\rm total}R_p=\frac{9}{14}\cdot\frac{2}{3}=\frac{3}{7}\text{ V}.
\]
Therefore
\[
I_{1\Omega}=\frac{V_p}{1}=\boxed{0.429\text{ A}}.
\]
Check: current in the $2\,\Omega$ branch is $0.214$ A; the branch currents add to $0.643$ A.
\end{exercise}

