The ammeter is in series so the component current passes through it. An ideal ammeter has $R_A=0$. The voltmeter is in parallel because voltage is measured between two points. An ideal voltmeter has $R_V\to\infty$ and draws no current.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A high-resistance voltmeter across points A and B reads 3 V. Replacing it by a low-resistance ammeter connects A and B almost directly by wire. What happens?

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \node at (0.1,1.1) {before:};
