\section{Current and Charge}

\textbf{What is current?} Current ($I$) is the \emph{rate of flow of electric charge}, i.e. how much charge passes a point in a wire per second. The unit is the ampere (A), where $1\text{ A}=1\text{ C s}^{-1}$.

\textbf{What does ``energy changes form'' mean?} When charge flows through a device such as a lamp, it transfers energy. The charge arrives with \emph{high electrical energy} and leaves with \emph{less}. The missing energy has been converted into light and heat. The charge itself is \textbf{not used up}; it keeps circulating. Total energy is conserved, just transformed into a different form.

\myskip
\textbf{Series circuit.} The diagram below shows a complete series circuit. Charge flows out of the positive ($+$) terminal of the battery, through the lamp, and back into the negative ($-$) terminal.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % ---- Circuit loop ----
    \draw (0,0.5) -- (0,3.2) -- (5,3.2) -- (5,0.5) -- (0,0.5);

    % ---- Battery on the left ----
    \draw[line width=1pt] (-0.45,3.2) -- (0.45,3.2);   % + plate (long)
    \draw[line width=2.6pt] (-0.22,0.5) -- (0.22,0.5);  % - plate (short thick)
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.5,3.2) {$+$};
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.5,0.5) {$-$};
    \node[left, align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.85,1.85) {battery};

    % ---- Lamp on the right ----
    \draw[fill=white] (5,1.85) circle (0.45);
    \draw (4.66,1.85) arc (180:360:0.34);
    \node[right, font=\footnotesize] at (5.55,1.85) {lamp};

    % ---- Current arrows (clockwise) ----
    \draw[->, blue!70!black, line width=1.3pt] (0.5,3.6) -- (4.5,3.6) node[midway, above, blue!70!black, font=\footnotesize] {$I$ (same everywhere)};

    % ---- Energy labels ----
    \node[anchor=south, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, red!70!black] at (5.0,3.6) {$E$ high};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\footnotesize, red!70!black] at (5.0,0.0) {$E$ low (light + heat)};

    % ---- Light arrow ----
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin] (5.5,2.45) .. controls +(0.4, 0.5) .. +(0.9, 0.4)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {light};
    % ---- Heat arrow ----
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin] (5.2,1.25) .. controls +(0.4, -0.45) .. +(0.9, -0.35)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {heat};
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Charge is conserved.} The same amount of charge that enters the lamp leaves it.
  \item \textbf{Current is the same everywhere} in a series circuit, i.e. the rate of charge flow does not slow down inside a component.
  \item \textbf{Energy changes form.} Electrical energy is transformed into light and thermal energy inside the lamp.
\end{itemize}

\myskip
\textbf{Direction of current.} Conventional current is defined as the direction that \emph{positive charge} would flow, i.e. from the positive ($+$) terminal of a battery, through the circuit, to the negative ($-$) terminal. In most circuits the actual moving charges are \emph{electrons}, which carry negative charge and therefore move in the \emph{opposite direction}.

