\section{Resistivity and Wire Dimensions}

For a uniform wire,
\[
\boxed{R=\rho\frac{\ell}{A}}, \qquad A=\pi r^2=\frac{\pi d^2}{4}.
\]
Resistivity $\rho$ is a material property at a stated temperature. The correct unit is $\Omega\,\mathrm m$.

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A wire of length $\ell$ and diameter $d$ has resistance $10\,\Omega$. A second wire of the same material has length $2\ell$ and diameter $0.5d$. Find its resistance.

The area factor is $(0.5)^2=0.25$. Hence
\[
\frac{R'}{R}=\frac{\ell'}{\ell}\frac{A}{A'}=2\left(\frac{1}{0.25}\right)=8,
\qquad R'=\boxed{80\,\Omega}.
\]
Halving diameter does not merely double resistance; it quarters area and therefore multiplies resistance by four.
\end{exercise}

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} Two rods of the same material are joined end-to-end. The first has length $2\ell$, diameter $2d$, and resistance $r$. The second has length $\ell$ and diameter $d$. Find the total resistance.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (0,0.35) rectangle (4,1.25);
  \draw[thick,fill=green!8] (4,0.55) rectangle (6,1.05);
  \draw[<->] (0,1.55) -- (4,1.55) node[midway,above] {$2\ell$};
  \draw[<->] (4,1.55) -- (6,1.55) node[midway,above] {$\ell$};
  \draw[<->] (-0.35,0.35) -- (-0.35,1.25) node[midway,left] {$2d$};
  \draw[<->] (6.35,0.55) -- (6.35,1.05) node[midway,right] {$d$};
  \node at (2,0.8) {$R_1=r$};
  \node at (5,0.8) {$R_2=?$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

For the first rod, $r\propto 2\ell/(2d)^2=\ell/(2d^2)$. For the second, $R_2\propto\ell/d^2$, so $R_2=2r$. They are end-to-end, hence in series:
\[
R_{\rm total}=r+2r=\boxed{3r}.
\]
\end{exercise}

\section{Ammeter and Voltmeter}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} How should current through a resistor and p.d. across it be measured?

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.2) -- (1.1,2.2);
  \draw[thick] (1.55,2.2) circle (0.45); \node at (1.55,2.2) {A};
  \draw[thick] (2,2.2) -- (2.7,2.2);
  \draw[thick] (2.7,1.85) rectangle (4.4,2.55) node[pos=.5] {$R$};
  \draw[thick] (4.4,2.2) -- (5.5,2.2) -- (5.5,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw[thick] (2.7,2.2) -- (2.7,3.5) -- (3.1,3.5);
  \draw[thick] (3.55,3.5) circle (0.45); \node at (3.55,3.5) {V};
  \draw[thick] (4,3.5) -- (4.4,3.5) -- (4.4,2.2);
  \draw[thick] (0.8,0.15) -- (0.8,-0.15);
  \draw[very thick] (1.15,0.3) -- (1.15,-0.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The ammeter is in series so the component current passes through it. An ideal ammeter has $R_A=0$. The voltmeter is in parallel because voltage is measured between two points. An ideal voltmeter has $R_V\to\infty$ and draws no current.
\end{exercise}

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A high-resistance voltmeter across points A and B reads 3 V. Replacing it by a low-resistance ammeter connects A and B almost directly by wire. What happens?

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \node at (0.1,1.1) {before:};
  \fill (1.2,1.1) circle (2pt) node[above] {A};
  \draw[thick] (1.2,1.1) -- (2.0,1.1);
  \draw[thick] (2.45,1.1) circle (0.45); \node at (2.45,1.1) {V};
  \draw[thick] (2.9,1.1) -- (3.7,1.1);
  \fill (3.7,1.1) circle (2pt) node[above] {B};
  \node[below] at (2.45,0.55) {3 V};
  \draw[->,thick] (4.15,1.1) -- (5.0,1.1);
  \node at (5.65,1.1) {after:};
  \fill (6.55,1.1) circle (2pt) node[above] {A};
  \draw[thick] (6.55,1.1) -- (7.35,1.1);
  \draw[thick] (7.8,1.1) circle (0.45); \node at (7.8,1.1) {A};
  \draw[thick] (8.25,1.1) -- (9.05,1.1);
  \fill (9.05,1.1) circle (2pt) node[above] {B};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The ammeter creates a short path between A and B. Their p.d. becomes approximately zero, and a large current may flow in that path. Never connect an ammeter directly across a cell or component.
