\section{Current and Charge}

\textbf{What is current?} Current ($I$) is the \emph{rate of flow of electric charge} — how much charge passes a point in a wire per second. The unit is the ampere (A), where $1\text{ A} = 1\text{ C s}^{-1}$.

\textbf{What does ``energy changes form'' mean?} When charge flows through a device such as a lamp, it transfers energy. The charge arrives with \emph{high electrical energy} and leaves with \emph{less} — the missing energy has been converted into light and heat. The charge itself is \textbf{not used up}; it keeps circulating. Total energy is conserved, just transformed into a different form.

\medskip
\textbf{Series circuit diagram.} The diagram below shows a complete series circuit. Charge flows out of the positive ($+$) terminal of the battery, through the lamp, and back into the negative ($-$) terminal.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % ---- Circuit loop ----
    \draw (0,0.5) -- (0,3.2) -- (5,3.2) -- (5,0.5) -- (0,0.5);

    % ---- Battery on the left ----
    \draw[line width=1pt] (-0.45,3.2) -- (0.45,3.2);   % + plate (long)
    \draw[line width=2.6pt] (-0.22,0.5) -- (0.22,0.5);  % - plate (short thick)
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.5,3.2) {$+$};
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.5,0.5) {$-$};
    \node[left, align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (-0.85,1.85) {battery};

    % ---- Lamp on the right ----
    \draw[fill=white] (5,1.85) circle (0.45);
    \draw (4.66,1.85) arc (180:360:0.34);
    \node[right, font=\footnotesize] at (5.55,1.85) {lamp};

    % ---- Current arrows (clockwise) ----
    \draw[->, blue!70!black, line width=1.3pt] (0.5,3.6) -- (4.5,3.6) node[midway, above, blue!70!black, font=\footnotesize] {$I$ (same everywhere)};

    % ---- Energy labels ----
    \node[anchor=south, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, red!70!black] at (5.0,3.6) {$E$ high};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\footnotesize, red!70!black] at (5.0,0.0) {$E$ low (light + heat)};

    % ---- Light arrow ----
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin] (5.5,2.45) .. controls +(0.4, 0.5) .. +(0.9, 0.4)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {light};
    % ---- Heat arrow ----
    \draw[->, red!70!black, very thin] (5.2,1.25) .. controls +(0.4, -0.45) .. +(0.9, -0.35)
      node[red!70!black, right, font=\footnotesize] {heat};
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Charge is conserved} — the same amount of charge that enters the lamp leaves it.
  \item \textbf{Current is the same everywhere} in a series circuit — the rate of charge flow does not slow down inside a component.
  \item \textbf{Energy changes form} — electrical energy is transformed into light and thermal energy inside the lamp.
\end{itemize}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A lamp carries a steady current of $0.30$ A for 2 minutes. Find the charge passing through it. Does the lamp use up charge?


\textbf{Solution.} Current is the rate of flow of charge:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{Q}{t}} \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad Q=It.
\]
Convert time first: $2$ min $=120$ s. Therefore
\[
Q=(0.30)(120)=\boxed{36\text{ C}}.
\]
The lamp does \emph{not} use up charge. In steady operation, 36 C enters the lamp and 36 C leaves it. The lamp transfers electrical energy to light and thermal energy.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} $1.88\times10^{18}$ electrons flow through a resistor each second. Find the current. (Electron charge magnitude $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C.)

\textbf{Solution.} Total charge per second is the number of electrons times the charge on each:
\[
\boxed{I=\frac{ne}{t}} \qquad I=\frac{(1.88\times10^{18})(1.60\times10^{-19})}{1}=\boxed{0.301\text{ A}}.
\]
Here $n$ is a pure count of electrons, so $Q=ne$ before dividing by time. Watch the powers of ten: $10^{18}\times10^{-19}=10^{-1}$.
\end{exercise}

\subsection{Direction of Current}

Conventional current is defined as the direction that \emph{positive charge} would flow — from the positive ($+$) terminal of a battery, through the circuit, to the negative ($-$) terminal. In most circuits, the actual moving charges are \emph{electrons}, which carry negative charge and therefore move in the \emph{opposite direction}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,line width=0.9pt]
  % ---- Circuit loop ----
  \draw (0,1.7) -- (0,3) -- (5,3) -- (5,0) -- (0,0) -- (0,1.3);

  % ---- Battery ----
  \draw[line width=1pt]   (-0.4,1.7) -- (0.4,1.7);
  \draw[line width=2.6pt] (-0.22,1.3) -- (0.22,1.3);
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.72) {$+$};
  \node[left] at (-0.42,1.28) {$-$};
  \node[left,align=center,font=\footnotesize] at (-0.75,1.5) {battery};

  % ---- Lamp ----
  \draw[fill=white] (5,1.5) circle (0.45);
  \draw (5-0.34,1.5) arc (180:360:0.34);
  \node[right,font=\footnotesize] at (5.6,1.5) {lamp};

  % ---- Conventional current: blue, out of + terminal, clockwise ----
  \draw[->,blue!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (0.6,3.42) -- (4.4,3.42);
  \node[blue!70!black,above,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,3.5) {conventional current $I$ (+ to $-$)};

  % ---- Electron flow: red, opposite direction ----
  \draw[->,red!70!black,line width=1.4pt] (4.4,2.58) -- (0.6,2.58);
  \node[red!70!black,below,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,2.5) {electron flow ($-$ to $+$)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=red!5,leftline=true,linecolor=red,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Common traps}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item Use the magnitude of electron charge, $e=1.60\times10^{-19}$ C, when finding current magnitude.
  \item Convert minutes to seconds and mA to A before using $Q=It$.
  \item Charges do not move more slowly after a resistor in a steady series circuit. The current is the same at every point in that path.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Parallel Circuits}

In a \textbf{parallel circuit}, components are connected side-by-side on separate branches. Charge splits between the branches — each branch receives part of the total charge. The total charge is still conserved.

\begin{center}
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm, >=stealth, line width=1pt]
    % ---- Top wire: left to right ----
    \draw[thick] (-2, 2) -- (0, 2);
    % ---- Bottom wire: left to right ----
    \draw[thick] (-2, 0) -- (0, 0);
    % ---- Right vertical return ----
    \draw[thick, ->] (4.5, 0) -- (4.5, 2) -- (3.5, 2) node[midway, above] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % ---- Junction dots ----
    \fill (0, 2) circle (0.1);
    \fill (4.5, 2) circle (0.1);
    \fill (0, 0) circle (0.1);
    \fill (4.5, 0) circle (0.1);

    % ---- Top branch: wire + lamp 1 ----
    \draw[thick] (0, 2) -- (1.8, 2) node[midway, above, font=\footnotesize] {$I_1$};
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.5cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.7, 2) {lamp 1};
    \draw[thick] (3.6, 2) -- (4.5, 2);

    % ---- Bottom branch: wire + lamp 2 ----
    \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (1.8, 0) node[midway, below, font=\footnotesize] {$I_2$};
    \node[draw, thick, fill=blue!10, minimum width=1.5cm, minimum height=0.9cm,
          align=center, font=\footnotesize] at (2.7, 0) {lamp 2};
    \draw[thick] (3.6, 0) -- (4.5, 0);

    % ---- Battery on the left vertical wire ----
    \draw[thick] (-2.0, 0.8) rectangle (-1.3, 1.2);
    \node[left, font=\footnotesize] at (-2.1, 1.0) {battery};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 1.2) {$+$};
    \node[left, font=\tiny] at (-2.1, 0.8) {$-$};

    % ---- Incoming current arrow ----
    \draw[thick, ->] (-2.8, 2) -- (-2.1, 2) node[midway, above, font=\footnotesize] {$I_{\text{total}}$};

    % ---- Annotations: current values ----
    \node[anchor=south, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.7, 2.9) {$I_1 = I_{\text{total}}/2$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\footnotesize\bfseries, blue!70!black] at (2.7, -0.3) {$I_2 = I_{\text{total}}/2$};

    % ---- Junction connection (bottom) ----
    \draw[thick] (0, 2) -- (0, 0);
    \draw[thick] (4.5, 2) -- (4.5, 0);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Each branch receives part of the total current: $I_1 + I_2 = I_{\text{total}}$.
  \item If both lamps are identical (equal resistance), the current splits equally: $I_1 = I_2 = I_{\text{total}}/2$.
  \item The current through each lamp is \emph{less} than the total current.
  \item In the water analogy: one pipe splits into two smaller pipes; the total water flow rate is conserved, but each branch carries a fraction.
\end{itemize}

\section{Potential, Potential Difference and EMF}

Potential is energy per unit positive charge at one point. Potential difference is the energy transferred per unit charge between two points:
\[
\boxed{V=\frac{W}{Q}} \qquad 1\text{ V}=1\text{ J C}^{-1}.
\]

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A cell supplies 12 J when 6 C passes through it. Find its emf. If a 1.0 V resistor carries 0.50 C, how much energy is converted in the resistor?

\textbf{Solution.}
\[
\mathcal E=\frac{W_{\rm supplied}}{Q}=\frac{12}{6}=\boxed{2.0\text{ V}},
\qquad
W_{\rm converted}=VQ=(1.0)(0.50)=\boxed{0.50\text{ J}}.
\]
A source gives energy to charge; a resistor converts electrical energy to other forms.
\end{exercise}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (8.5,0) node[right] {position around circuit};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {potential};
  \draw[very thick] (0.7,0.7) -- (1.7,0.7);
  \draw[very thick,->] (1.7,0.7) -- (1.7,3.7) node[midway,right] {cell: rise $\mathcal E$};
  \draw[very thick] (1.7,3.7) -- (4.5,3.7);
  \draw[very thick,->] (4.5,3.7) -- (4.5,2.2) node[midway,right] {$V_1$};
  \draw[very thick] (4.5,2.2) -- (6.5,2.2);
  \draw[very thick,->] (6.5,2.2) -- (6.5,0.7) node[midway,right] {$V_2$};
  \draw[very thick] (6.5,0.7) -- (8,0.7);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

For a complete loop with an ideal cell,
\[
\boxed{\text{total emf}=\text{sum of potential drops}}.
\]
A component causes no potential change if it is bypassed by an ideal wire.

\needspace{8\baselineskip}
\begin{exercise}[nobreak=true]
\textbf{Question.} A 6 V supply is connected across three identical $2\,\Omega$ resistors in series. Point B, at the negative terminal, is earthed. Find the potentials after the first and second resistors when moving from the positive terminal.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,1.7) -- (0.6,1.7);
  \draw[thick] (0.6,1.4) rectangle (2.0,2.0) node[pos=.5] {$2\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick] (2.0,1.7) -- (2.6,1.7);
  \draw[thick] (2.6,1.4) rectangle (4.0,2.0) node[pos=.5] {$2\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick] (4.0,1.7) -- (4.6,1.7);
  \draw[thick] (4.6,1.4) rectangle (6.0,2.0) node[pos=.5] {$2\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick] (6.0,1.7) -- (6.6,1.7) -- (6.6,0) -- (1.0,0);
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0.65,0);
  \draw[thick] (0.65,-0.18) -- (0.65,0.18);
  \draw[very thick] (1.0,-0.32) -- (1.0,0.32);
  \node[below] at (0.82,-0.35) {6 V};
  \node[above] at (0,1.7) {$+6$ V};
  \node[above] at (2.3,1.7) {$P$};
  \node[above] at (4.3,1.7) {$Q$};
  \node[right] at (6.6,0.8) {$B$};
  \draw[thick] (6.6,0) -- (6.6,-0.35);
  \draw[thick] (6.25,-0.35) -- (6.95,-0.35);
  \draw[thick] (6.38,-0.5) -- (6.82,-0.5);
  \draw[thick] (6.5,-0.65) -- (6.7,-0.65);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} Earthing sets $V_B=0$, but does not by itself change the currents. The three equal resistors share 6 V equally, so each drop is 2 V. The positive terminal is at $+6$ V; the next points are $+4$ V and $+2$ V, then B is 0 V.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A 6 V battery and a 12 V battery are connected in the same series loop with a $2\,\Omega$ and a $4\,\Omega$ resistor, arranged so the two batteries drive current the same way round. Find the loop current, then the p.d. across the point pair $PQ$ that spans the $2\,\Omega$ resistor and the 6 V battery together.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.2) -- (0.7,2.2);
  \draw[thick] (0.7,1.85) rectangle (2.2,2.55) node[pos=.5] {$2\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick] (2.2,2.2) -- (3.0,2.2);
  \draw[thick] (3.0,1.85) rectangle (4.5,2.55) node[pos=.5] {$4\,\Omega$};
  \draw[thick] (4.5,2.2) -- (5.5,2.2) -- (5.5,0) -- (4.75,0);
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0.85,0);
  \draw[very thick] (0.85,-0.32) -- (0.85,0.32);
  \draw[thick] (1.2,-0.18) -- (1.2,0.18);
  \draw[thick] (1.2,0) -- (3.0,0);
  \draw[very thick] (3.0,-0.32) -- (3.0,0.32);
  \draw[thick] (3.35,-0.18) -- (3.35,0.18);
  \draw[thick] (3.35,0) -- (5.5,0);
  \node[below] at (1.03,-0.38) {6 V};
  \node[below] at (3.18,-0.38) {12 V};
  \fill (0,0) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$P$};
  \fill (2.2,2.2) circle (2pt) node[above] {$Q$};
  \draw[->,blue!70!black,thick] (1.0,2.85) -- (4.5,2.85) node[midway,above] {$I$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} When sources aid each other their emfs add; when they oppose, subtract. Here they aid, so the net emf is $6+12=18$ V around a total resistance $2+4=6\,\Omega$:
\[
I=\frac{\text{net emf}}{\text{total }R}=\frac{18}{6}=3.0\text{ A}.
\]
Across $PQ$, the $2\,\Omega$ resistor drops $IR=3.0\times2=6$ V while the 6 V battery raises the potential by 6 V in the opposite sense, so
\[
V_{PQ}=6-6=\boxed{0\text{ V}}.
\]
Always fix a direction round the loop first, then add emfs that push that way and subtract those that push back.
\end{exercise}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=green!5,leftline=true,linecolor=green!60!black,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Cells and capacity}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item Identical cells \textbf{in series} add emf ($n$ cells give $n\mathcal E$). \textbf{In parallel} the emf is unchanged (still $\mathcal E$), but they last longer.
  \item Battery capacity in ampere-hours is a charge: $1\text{ A h}=1\text{ A}\times3600\text{ s}=3600$ C. Energy available is $W=VQ=V(It)$. Example: a 11.1 V, 4.8 A h pack stores $11.1\times4.8\times3600\approx1.9\times10^{5}$ J.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\section{Terminal Potential Difference}

An ideal source has no internal resistance, so terminal p.d. equals emf. A real source has internal resistance $r$:
\[
\boxed{V_{\rm terminal}=\mathcal E-Ir}.
\]

\needspace{10\baselineskip}
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A cell of emf 3.0 V and internal resistance $0.50\,\Omega$ supplies 1.0 A. Find its terminal p.d.

\textbf{Solution.}
\[
V_{\rm terminal}=3.0-(1.0)(0.50)=\boxed{2.5\text{ V}}.
\]
The missing 0.5 V is the p.d. across the internal resistance. With no current, $Ir=0$ and the terminal p.d. returns to the emf.
\end{exercise}

\section{Resistance and Ohm's Law}

For any component at a particular operating point,
\[
\boxed{R=\frac{V}{I}}.
\]
An \textbf{ohmic} component at constant temperature has constant $R$, so $V\propto I$. Not every conductor is ohmic: a filament heats up, causing its resistance to change.

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A 1.5 V p.d. is applied across a $2.0\,\Omega$ lamp. Find the current, treating its resistance as 2.0 $\Omega$ at this operating point.
\[
I=\frac{V}{R}=\frac{1.5}{2.0}=\boxed{0.75\text{ A}}.
\]
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A resistance wire gives the straight line below through the origin. Find its resistance, and state how the line changes for a shorter wire of the same material and radius.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$I$/A};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$V$/V};
  \draw[very thick,blue!70!black] (0,0) -- (3.2,3.2);
  \draw[dashed] (2,0) node[below] {\footnotesize $2.0$} -- (2,2) -- (0,2) node[left] {\footnotesize $4.0$};
  \draw[very thick,red!70!black,dotted] (0,0) -- (3.4,1.7);
  \node[red!70!black] at (3.2,1.15) {\footnotesize shorter};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} The line is straight through the origin, so the slope is the resistance. Reading a convenient point, $V=4.0$ V at $I=2.0$ A:
\[
R=\frac{V}{I}=\frac{4.0}{2.0}=\boxed{2.0\ \Omega}.
\]
A shorter wire has smaller $R=\rho\ell/A$, so the slope is smaller (the dotted line). Because $V=IR$, the graph still passes through the origin.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A metal wire is connected to a fixed $5.0$ V supply. Its resistance is $5\,\Omega$ at $25^\circ$C and rises to $12\,\Omega$ at $200^\circ$C. The ammeter reads $1.0$ A at $25^\circ$C. Find the reading at $200^\circ$C.

\textbf{Solution.} The supply voltage across the wire stays $V=IR=(1.0)(5)=5.0$ V. At the higher temperature the resistance is larger, so
\[
I=\frac{V}{R}=\frac{5.0}{12}=\boxed{0.42\text{ A}}.
\]
Hotter metal means more resistance and, at fixed voltage, less current. This is why a filament lamp is \emph{not} ohmic.
\end{exercise}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=yellow!10,leftline=true,linecolor=yellow!60!black,linewidth=2pt]
\textbf{Reading graphs correctly}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item On a $V$-against-$I$ graph, the slope is $R$ only for a straight line through the origin.
  \item For a non-ohmic component, the operating resistance at a point is the ratio $V/I$, not automatically the tangent slope.
  \item A filament lamp's resistance increases as it becomes hotter.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\section{Resistivity and Wire Dimensions}

For a uniform wire,
\[
\boxed{R=\rho\frac{\ell}{A}}, \qquad A=\pi r^2=\frac{\pi d^2}{4}.
\]
Resistivity $\rho$ is a material property at a stated temperature. The correct unit is $\Omega\,\mathrm m$.

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A wire of length $\ell$ and diameter $d$ has resistance $10\,\Omega$. A second wire of the same material has length $2\ell$ and diameter $0.5d$. Find its resistance.

The area factor is $(0.5)^2=0.25$. Hence
\[
\frac{R'}{R}=\frac{\ell'}{\ell}\frac{A}{A'}=2\left(\frac{1}{0.25}\right)=8,
\qquad R'=\boxed{80\,\Omega}.
\]
Halving diameter does not merely double resistance; it quarters area and therefore multiplies resistance by four.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} Two rods of the same material are joined end-to-end. The first has length $2\ell$, diameter $2d$, and resistance $r$. The second has length $\ell$ and diameter $d$. Find the total resistance.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (0,0.35) rectangle (4,1.25);
  \draw[thick,fill=green!8] (4,0.55) rectangle (6,1.05);
  \draw[<->] (0,1.55) -- (4,1.55) node[midway,above] {$2\ell$};
  \draw[<->] (4,1.55) -- (6,1.55) node[midway,above] {$\ell$};
  \draw[<->] (-0.35,0.35) -- (-0.35,1.25) node[midway,left] {$2d$};
  \draw[<->] (6.35,0.55) -- (6.35,1.05) node[midway,right] {$d$};
  \node at (2,0.8) {$R_1=r$};
  \node at (5,0.8) {$R_2=?$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

For the first rod, $r\propto 2\ell/(2d)^2=\ell/(2d^2)$. For the second, $R_2\propto\ell/d^2$, so $R_2=2r$. They are end-to-end, hence in series:
\[
R_{\rm total}=r+2r=\boxed{3r}.
\]
\end{exercise}


